Apple will likely consolidate its high-end <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> lineup around touchscreen models, according to <em>Bloomberg</em>'s <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-11-01/apple-touch-screen-macbook-pro-impressions-software-division-gaming-shake-up">Mark Gurman</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/10/14-inch-MacBook-Pro-Keyboard.jpg" alt="" width="1428" height="814" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1023443" /><br/>
Writing in his latest <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-11-01/apple-touch-screen-macbook-pro-impressions-software-division-gaming-shake-up">"Power On" newsletter</a>, Gurman questioned whether Apple would keep updating its high-end &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; models without touchscreens. The company has retained the mini-LED M5 Pro and M5 Max models alongside the new touchscreen versions, which cost &#36;400 more.<br/>
<br/>
Gurman cited the resources required to support parallel product lines across development, production, and promotion. Given the price difference, he expects the two offerings to converge, although he did not specify when Apple might stop developing the non-touch models.<br/>
<br/>
Gurman added that Apple plans to introduce another generation of touchscreen MacBook Pros in 2027. These machines will use M7 Pro and M7 Max processors with additional hardware capabilities for AI. He <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/15/macbook-pro-m7-late-2027/">previously said</a> the chip upgrade would arrive toward the end of 2027.<br/>
<br/>
The redesign will also reach the entry-level 14-inch &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;, according to Gurman. Apple plans to offer an M7 version with reduced weight, a thinner enclosure, and narrower borders around the display, similar to the new high-end models. Gurman <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/07/01/redesigned-m7-macbook-pro-2027/">first reported</a> plans for the redesigned base model in July, when he outlined a potential launch in the first half of 2027.<br/>
<br/>
Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/28/apple-new-macbook-pro/">unveiled</a> its first touchscreen MacBook Pros last week, retaining the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/03/apple-debuts-m5-pro-and-m5-max-chips/">M5 Pro and M5 Max chips</a> introduced in March. The redesign pairs <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> displays and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/dynamic-island/">Dynamic Island</a> with a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/28/oled-macbook-pro-size-weight/">lighter, slightly thinner enclosure</a> and a revised cooling system that channels warm air away from the screen.<br/>
